From: Manuel de Brito Fontes <aledbf@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 14:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 10/11] x86/early-quirks: Skip the PCI scan in a KVM guest

early_quirks() scans bus 0, and the buses behind its bridges, through
configuration mechanism #1 for the chipsets, GPUs and NICs in
early_qrk[]. Every read is two port I/O exits to the VMM, the address
written to 0xcf8 and the data read at 0xcfc, and on QEMU's q35 neither
is handled in the kernel: on this machine's bus, 7 functions and 25
empty slots, that is over a hundred exits.

None of the entries matches what this machine has: the ICH9 host bridge
(8086:29c0, class 0600) and LPC bridge (8086:2918, class 0601), and the
virtio devices (1af4:1042, 1044, 1045, 1048, 1058), read from
/sys/bus/pci/devices in a guest. It has no bridge to recurse into and
passes no device through.

Return before the scan when kvm_para_available(). This is not
pci=noearly, with which this machine's guest does not boot: that also
keeps pci_arch_init() from setting up MMCONFIG early, and every other
early_pci_allowed() user from running. Only the scan is skipped here.

Measured in a KVM guest on a laptop, not nested (QEMU q35, two vCPUs,
two runs of 10 boots each, interleaved; a lead, not the bench fleet):
the time from "ACPI: Reserving WAET table memory" to "ACPI: LAPIC_NMI"
0.44 -> 0.07 and 0.32 -> 0.06 ms p50. Nested on the bench fleet, where
an exit to userspace costs more, the gap was 2.1 ms (lab run
44563496308).

A guest given a device that early_qrk[] lists, such as an Intel GPU
passed through, needs the scan. This kernel runs only on this machine.

Local patch for this machine. Not upstream.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 arch/x86/kernel/early-quirks.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/early-quirks.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/early-quirks.c
@@ -28,6 +28,7 @@
 #include <asm/gart.h>
 #include <asm/irq_remapping.h>
 #include <asm/early_ioremap.h>
+#include <asm/kvm_para.h>
 
 static void __init fix_hypertransport_config(int num, int slot, int func)
 {
@@ -808,5 +809,16 @@ void __init early_quirks(void)
 	if (!early_pci_allowed())
 		return;
 
+	/*
+	 * Every quirk above is for a chipset, a GPU or a NIC on real
+	 * hardware, and a KVM guest's emulated devices match none of them.
+	 * The scan costs a KVM guest two port I/O exits to the VMM for
+	 * every configuration read, a hundred or more on a QEMU q35 board.
+	 * A device passed through to a guest could match one (an Intel
+	 * GPU's stolen memory); a guest that has one must not skip it.
+	 */
+	if (kvm_para_available())
+		return;
+
 	early_pci_scan_bus(0);
 }
